Name: sessionCookieSecure

Type: property

Syntax: set the sessionCookieSecure to {true | false}

Syntax: get the sessionCookieSecure

Summary:
Specifies whether the cookie that stores the session id is sent with the
Secure attribute, so that browsers return it over HTTPS only.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: server

Example:
set the sessionCookieSecure to true
start session

Example:
if $_SERVER["HTTPS"] is "on" then set the sessionCookieSecure to true

Value (bool):
The <sessionCookieSecure> is true or false. By default, it is false.

Description:
Use the <sessionCookieSecure> property on a site served over HTTPS, so
that the session id is never sent over an unencrypted connection.

When a session is started, a cookie is created in the browser to
identify the ongoing session. By default that cookie has the HttpOnly
attribute alone, as it always has. When the <sessionCookieSecure> is
true, the cookie also has the Secure attribute: the browser then sends
it back with requests over HTTPS only, and not at all to a page of the
site that is requested over plain HTTP, where a session would start
anew on every request.

Set the <sessionCookieSecure> before the <start session> command (and
before <delete session>, whose expired cookie takes the same
attributes). It is not saved anywhere: a site that wants it sets it on
every request, for example in a file every page includes.

>*Note:* You do not need to alter any of the session properties in order
> to start and use sessions. They provide a way for advanced users to
> configure the way session work.

>*Note:* The sessionCookieSecure property can be read and set in every
> engine, and has an effect only when running in CGI mode (Server).

References: sessionCookieSameSite (property),
sessionCookieName (property), sessionSavePath (property),
sessionLifetime (property), sessionID (property),
start session (command), delete session (command), put cookie (command)
